Reinforcement learning (RL) has recently advanced significantly, particularly in domains like video games and robotics, where agents learn to achieve goals through trial and error. However, most RL research has focused on single agents, whereas many practical applications require multiple agents to collaborate toward a shared objective. Multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) has emerged as a promising solution for these complex scenarios. Nevertheless, the interdependence of agents in MARL complicates the learning process, as the actions of one agent influence the rewards received by others.

To tackle these challenges, we introduce a new parallel training framework for MARL. This framework utilizes a parallel training algorithm that trains individual policies simultaneously while ensuring the overall objective aligns with each agent's sub-objective. Additionally, we have developed a parallel execution algorithm to enable agents to execute actions concurrently. We also address the issue of data consistency arising from this simultaneous action execution. Our method is compatible with any existing RL algorithm, and we have created a parallel training library to support it. We validate the framework's effectiveness through experiments on various MARL tasks, including multi-agent card games, multi-agent grid worlds, and multi-agent card games. Our results show that the proposed method can speed up training by up to 10 times.

